the AI can take over.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.270
And because of that little smartphone

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:12.210
they can all look at becoming
infotainers themselves

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.770
and earn on their
entertainment and their data.

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:16.280
That's really exciting.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:17.230
- I agree that's great.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.913
So Neil, I want to close
with you on this one.

00:13:18.913 --> 00:13:22.220
You're very modest, but
you are a master inventor,

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.410
you are a master prognosticator.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.300
What do you see happening here?

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.030
- I want to start by just
trying to sum it up that

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:31.060
machines are about productivity

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.490
and people are about creativity.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:35.623
There are things that machines
do better than people,

00:13:35.623 --> 00:13:38.310
there are things people do
much better than machines.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.296
And we have to get to that line.

00:13:40.296 --> 00:13:41.450
And I think what's going on today

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:43.270
is people are surprised
by some of the things

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:44.910
that machines are good at.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:45.980
People say, well, okay,

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.705
driving a taxi or a bus, that's one thing.

00:13:48.705 --> 00:13:51.940
But how could an AI do
something a lawyer could do?

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.690
If you think about some of the tasks that

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:56.370
lawyers do coming out of law school,

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:59.370
they're very productive types of tasks.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:00.203
And the goal is not to

00:14:00.203 --> 00:14:02.067
'we're gonna replace all
those guys with that',

00:14:02.067 --> 00:14:03.390
but we're gonna free their time up

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:04.730
to do more complex work,

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:06.440
more creative type of work.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.260
Maybe that's case strategy,

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:09.540
maybe that's more engagement

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:12.440
and understanding the
client or what's going on.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:14.960
I think what we really have
to do deal with, again,

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:18.160
as a society is
understanding how our work,

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.220
how our lives are going
to be transformed by this.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:24.470
What can we do that's
more, that's more creative?

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.610
And I don't think this is
a unique challenge for us.

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:30.230
You know, technology
has always been there,

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:31.230
reshaping our lives.

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:34.700
I think it's the change is
coming faster and faster.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:36.380
And I think that's what
people are struggling with

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:37.840
is the stuff we're talking about

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.540
is not 20 years away.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.080
In some cases it's not
even five years away,

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.520
it's already here.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:43.890
What do we do?

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.460
- So this shift that we're talking about

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:47.580
opens up a subject.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:48.540
And this is a unique panel.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.070
'Cause we can go from deep tech

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.560
to diversity and culture.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:52.980
And so I wanna hand this one off to Denise

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.730
'cause obviously this
queues you up beautifully

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.494
